<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
Host names returned by a reverse DNS lookup of an IP address are controlled by whoever controls the DNS (PTR)
records for that address. An attacker can make their own IP address resolve to a trusted host name, so a condition
that compares a reverse DNS host name with an expected value must not decide whether a sensitive action (such as
logging a user in) is performed.
</p>
<p>
This query extends <code>cs/user-controlled-bypass</code> with reverse DNS sources it does not model:
<code>Dns.GetHostEntry(IPAddress)</code> and <code>Dns.GetHostEntryAsync(IPAddress)</code>. It also models
<code>Dns.GetHostByAddress</code>, which overlaps with the standard query for constant comparisons, and follows
comparisons of the host name against non-constant values, such as a configured host name.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not use reverse DNS to make security decisions. Authenticate the peer with a cryptographic mechanism (for
example TLS client certificates), or, if network location must be used, compare the peer IP address against an
allow-list of addresses.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the user is logged in only when the reverse DNS host name of the peer matches a trusted
host name. An attacker who controls the PTR record of their address can bypass this check.
</p>

<sample language="csharp"><![CDATA[
IPHostEntry hostInfo = Dns.GetHostEntry(peerAddress);
if (hostInfo.HostName.Equals("trusted.example.com"))
{
    Login(user, password);
}
]]></sample>

<p>
Instead, base the decision on the peer address itself:
</p>

<sample language="csharp"><![CDATA[
if (AllowedAddresses.Contains(peerAddress))
{
    Login(user, password);
}
]]></sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/350.html">CWE-350: Reliance on Reverse DNS Resolution for a Security-Critical Action</a>.
</li>
<li>
MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html">CWE-807: Reliance on Untrusted Inputs in a Security Decision</a>.
</li>
<li>
MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/247.html">CWE-247: Reliance on DNS Lookups in a Security Decision</a>.
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
